1
00:00:02,078 --> 00:00:03,800
Chapter 39.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:09,160
I was three-and-twenty years of age. Not another word had I heard to enlighten me on the subject of

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:16,000
my expectations, and my twenty-third birthday was a week gone. We had left Barnard’s Inn

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,758
more than a year, and lived in the Temple. Our chambers were in Garden-court, down by the river.

5
00:00:22,758 --> 00:00:29,038
Mr. Pocket and I had for some time parted company as to our original relations, though we continued

6
00:00:29,038 --> 00:00:35,641
on the best terms. Notwithstanding my inability to settle to anything,—which I hope arose out of

7
00:00:35,641 --> 00:00:41,718
the restless and incomplete tenure on which I held my means,—I had a taste for reading,

8
00:00:42,718 --> 00:00:49,438
and read regularly so many hours a day. That matter of Herbert’s was still progressing,

9
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:55,160
and everything with me was as I have brought it down to the close of the last preceding chapter.

10
00:00:55,160 --> 00:01:01,519
Business had taken Herbert on a journey to Marseilles. I was alone, and had a dull sense of

11
00:01:01,519 --> 00:01:09,718
being alone. Dispirited and anxious, long hoping that to-morrow or next week would clear my way,

12
00:01:09,718 --> 00:01:16,358
and long disappointed, I sadly missed the cheerful face and ready response of my friend.

13
00:01:16,358 --> 00:01:23,199
It was wretched weather; stormy and wet, stormy and wet; and mud, mud, mud, deep

14
00:01:23,199 --> 00:01:29,398
in all the streets. Day after day, a vast heavy veil had been driving over London from the East,

15
00:01:29,398 --> 00:01:36,116
and it drove still, as if in the East there were an eternity of cloud and wind. So furious had

16
00:01:36,116 --> 00:01:41,116
been the gusts, that high buildings in town had had the lead stripped off their roofs;

17
00:01:41,116 --> 00:01:47,160
and in the country, trees had been torn up, and sails of windmills carried away; and gloomy

18
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:53,758
accounts had come in from the coast, of shipwreck and death. Violent blasts of rain had accompanied

19
00:01:53,758 --> 00:02:00,957
these rages of wind, and the day just closed as I sat down to read had been the worst of all.

20
00:02:00,957 --> 00:02:04,839
Alterations have been made in that part of the Temple since that time,

21
00:02:04,839 --> 00:02:11,879
and it has not now so lonely a character as it had then, nor is it so exposed to the river. We

22
00:02:11,879 --> 00:02:17,718
lived at the top of the last house, and the wind rushing up the river shook the house that night,

23
00:02:17,718 --> 00:02:24,839
like discharges of cannon, or breakings of a sea. When the rain came with it and dashed against the

24
00:02:24,839 --> 00:02:30,277
windows, I thought, raising my eyes to them as they rocked, that I might have fancied myself in

25
00:02:30,277 --> 00:02:36,160
a storm-beaten lighthouse. Occasionally, the smoke came rolling down the chimney as though it could

26
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:41,639
not bear to go out into such a night; and when I set the doors open and looked down the staircase,

27
00:02:41,641 --> 00:02:45,957
the staircase lamps were blown out; and when I shaded my face with my hands and

28
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:50,398
looked through the black windows (opening them ever so little was out of the question

29
00:02:50,398 --> 00:02:55,838
in the teeth of such wind and rain), I saw that the lamps in the court were blown out,

30
00:02:55,839 --> 00:03:00,800
and that the lamps on the bridges and the shore were shuddering, and that the coal-fires in barges

31
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:06,438
on the river were being carried away before the wind like red-hot splashes in the rain.

32
00:03:06,438 --> 00:03:13,559
I read with my watch upon the table, purposing to close my book at eleven o’clock. As I shut it,

33
00:03:13,559 --> 00:03:18,839
Saint Paul’s, and all the many church-clocks in the City—some leading, some accompanying,

34
00:03:18,839 --> 00:03:25,438
some following—struck that hour. The sound was curiously flawed by the wind; and I was listening,

35
00:03:25,438 --> 00:03:30,800
and thinking how the wind assailed and tore it, when I heard a footstep on the stair.

36
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:35,917
What nervous folly made me start, and awfully connect it with the footstep of my dead sister,

37
00:03:35,917 --> 00:03:40,718
matters not. It was past in a moment, and I listened again,

38
00:03:40,718 --> 00:03:45,277
and heard the footstep stumble in coming on. Remembering then,

39
00:03:45,277 --> 00:03:49,839
that the staircase-lights were blown out, I took up my reading-lamp and went out to the

40
00:03:49,839 --> 00:03:56,199
stair-head. Whoever was below had stopped on seeing my lamp, for all was quiet.

41
00:03:56,199 --> 00:04:00,597
“There is some one down there, is there not?” I called out, looking down.

42
00:04:00,597 --> 00:04:04,116
“Yes,” said a voice from the darkness beneath.

43
00:04:04,116 --> 00:04:06,038
“What floor do you want?”

44
00:04:06,038 --> 00:04:08,436
“The top. Mr. Pip.”

45
00:04:08,438 --> 00:04:11,438
“That is my name.—There is nothing the matter?”

46
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:17,199
“Nothing the matter,” returned the voice. And the man came on.

47
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:22,879
I stood with my lamp held out over the stair-rail, and he came slowly within its light. It was a

48
00:04:22,879 --> 00:04:28,839
shaded lamp, to shine upon a book, and its circle of light was very contracted; so that he was in

49
00:04:28,839 --> 00:04:35,078
it for a mere instant, and then out of it. In the instant, I had seen a face that was strange to me,

50
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:41,000
looking up with an incomprehensible air of being touched and pleased by the sight of me.

51
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,438
Moving the lamp as the man moved, I made out that he was substantially dressed, but roughly,

52
00:04:46,438 --> 00:04:54,000
like a voyager by sea. That he had long iron-grey hair. That his age was about sixty. That he was a

53
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,319
muscular man, strong on his legs, and that he was browned and hardened by exposure to

54
00:04:59,319 --> 00:05:05,557
weather. As he ascended the last stair or two, and the light of my lamp included us both,

55
00:05:05,559 --> 00:05:11,160
I saw, with a stupid kind of amazement, that he was holding out both his hands to me.

56
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:13,597
“Pray what is your business?” I asked him.

57
00:05:13,597 --> 00:05:19,758
“My business?” he repeated, pausing. “Ah! Yes. I will explain my business, by your leave.”

58
00:05:19,758 --> 00:05:21,718
“Do you wish to come in?”

59
00:05:21,718 --> 00:05:28,917
“Yes,” he replied; “I wish to come in, master.” I had asked him the question inhospitably enough,

60
00:05:28,917 --> 00:05:32,597
for I resented the sort of bright and gratified recognition that still shone

61
00:05:32,597 --> 00:05:39,641
in his face. I resented it, because it seemed to imply that he expected me to respond to it.

62
00:05:39,641 --> 00:05:44,160
But I took him into the room I had just left, and, having set the lamp on the table, asked

63
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:50,800
him as civilly as I could to explain himself. He looked about him with the strangest air,—an

64
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:56,078
air of wondering pleasure, as if he had some part in the things he admired,—and he pulled

65
00:05:56,078 --> 00:06:02,641
off a rough outer coat, and his hat. Then, I saw that his head was furrowed and bald, and that

66
00:06:02,641 --> 00:06:09,038
the long iron-grey hair grew only on its sides. But, I saw nothing that in the least explained

67
00:06:09,038 --> 00:06:16,957
him. On the contrary, I saw him next moment, once more holding out both his hands to me.

68
00:06:16,957 --> 00:06:21,917
“What do you mean?” said I, half suspecting him to be mad.

69
00:06:21,917 --> 00:06:27,879
He stopped in his looking at me, and slowly rubbed his right hand over his head. “It’s

70
00:06:27,879 --> 00:06:34,116
disapinting to a man,” he said, in a coarse broken voice, “arter having looked for’ard so distant,

71
00:06:34,116 --> 00:06:38,639
and come so fur; but you’re not to blame for that,—neither on us is

72
00:06:38,641 --> 00:06:43,917
to blame for that. I’ll speak in half a minute. Give me half a minute, please.”

73
00:06:43,917 --> 00:06:46,879
He sat down on a chair that stood before the fire,

74
00:06:46,879 --> 00:06:53,480
and covered his forehead with his large brown veinous hands. I looked at him attentively then,

75
00:06:53,480 --> 00:06:57,917
and recoiled a little from him; but I did not know him.

76
00:06:57,917 --> 00:07:02,438
“There’s no one nigh,” said he, looking over his shoulder; “is there?”

77
00:07:02,438 --> 00:07:05,438
“Why do you, a stranger coming into my rooms at this time of the night,

78
00:07:05,438 --> 00:07:07,559
ask that question?” said I.

79
00:07:07,559 --> 00:07:12,718
“You’re a game one,” he returned, shaking his head at me with a deliberate affection,

80
00:07:12,718 --> 00:07:17,597
at once most unintelligible and most exasperating; “I’m glad you’ve grow’d up,

81
00:07:17,597 --> 00:07:23,597
a game one! But don’t catch hold of me. You’d be sorry arterwards to have done it.”

82
00:07:23,597 --> 00:07:29,358
I relinquished the intention he had detected, for I knew him! Even yet I could not recall a

83
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:36,436
single feature, but I knew him! If the wind and the rain had driven away the intervening years,

84
00:07:36,438 --> 00:07:41,879
had scattered all the intervening objects, had swept us to the churchyard where we first stood

85
00:07:41,879 --> 00:07:46,879
face to face on such different levels, I could not have known my convict more distinctly than

86
00:07:46,879 --> 00:07:53,116
I knew him now as he sat in the chair before the fire. No need to take a file from his pocket and

87
00:07:53,116 --> 00:07:59,436
show it to me; no need to take the handkerchief from his neck and twist it round his head; no

88
00:07:59,438 --> 00:08:05,277
need to hug himself with both his arms, and take a shivering turn across the room, looking back at

89
00:08:05,277 --> 00:08:11,237
me for recognition. I knew him before he gave me one of those aids, though, a moment before,

90
00:08:11,839 --> 00:08:16,160
I had not been conscious of remotely suspecting his identity.

91
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:22,718
He came back to where I stood, and again held out both his hands. Not knowing what

92
00:08:22,718 --> 00:08:27,559
to do,—for, in my astonishment I had lost my self-possession,—I reluctantly gave him

93
00:08:27,559 --> 00:08:36,038
my hands. He grasped them heartily, raised them to his lips, kissed them, and still held them.

94
00:08:36,038 --> 00:08:43,398
“You acted noble, my boy,” said he. “Noble, Pip! And I have never forgot it!”

95
00:08:43,398 --> 00:08:47,358
At a change in his manner as if he were even going to embrace me,

96
00:08:47,358 --> 00:08:50,957
I laid a hand upon his breast and put him away.

97
00:08:50,957 --> 00:08:57,436
“Stay!” said I. “Keep off! If you are grateful to me for what I did when I was a little child,

98
00:08:57,438 --> 00:09:02,277
I hope you have shown your gratitude by mending your way of life. If you have come

99
00:09:02,277 --> 00:09:08,597
here to thank me, it was not necessary. Still, however you have found me out,

100
00:09:08,597 --> 00:09:11,398
there must be something good in the feeling that has brought you here,

101
00:09:11,398 --> 00:09:17,800
and I will not repulse you; but surely you must understand that—I—”

102
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,038
My attention was so attracted by the singularity of his fixed look at me,

103
00:09:22,038 --> 00:09:25,038
that the words died away on my tongue.

104
00:09:25,038 --> 00:09:30,000
“You was a-saying,” he observed, when we had confronted one another in silence,

105
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,199
“that surely I must understand. What, surely must I understand?”

106
00:09:35,199 --> 00:09:39,557
“That I cannot wish to renew that chance intercourse with you of long ago,

107
00:09:39,559 --> 00:09:44,160
under these different circumstances. I am glad to believe you have repented and

108
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:50,597
recovered yourself. I am glad to tell you so. I am glad that, thinking I deserve to be thanked,

109
00:09:50,597 --> 00:09:58,358
you have come to thank me. But our ways are different ways, none the less. You are wet,

110
00:09:58,358 --> 00:10:03,557
and you look weary. Will you drink something before you go?”

111
00:10:03,559 --> 00:10:08,957
He had replaced his neckerchief loosely, and had stood, keenly observant of me,

112
00:10:08,957 --> 00:10:14,358
biting a long end of it. “I think,” he answered, still with the end at his

113
00:10:14,358 --> 00:10:21,597
mouth and still observant of me, “that I will drink (I thank you) afore I go.”

114
00:10:21,597 --> 00:10:26,641
There was a tray ready on a side-table. I brought it to the table near the fire,

115
00:10:26,641 --> 00:10:32,319
and asked him what he would have? He touched one of the bottles without looking at it or speaking,

116
00:10:32,319 --> 00:10:38,436
and I made him some hot rum and water. I tried to keep my hand steady while I did so,

117
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:41,917
but his look at me as he leaned back in his chair with the long draggled end of

118
00:10:41,917 --> 00:10:47,116
his neckerchief between his teeth—evidently forgotten—made my hand very difficult to

119
00:10:47,116 --> 00:10:54,398
master. When at last I put the glass to him, I saw with amazement that his eyes were full of tears.

120
00:10:54,398 --> 00:11:00,160
Up to this time I had remained standing, not to disguise that I wished him gone. But I

121
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:07,358
was softened by the softened aspect of the man, and felt a touch of reproach. “I hope,” said I,

122
00:11:07,358 --> 00:11:11,038
hurriedly putting something into a glass for myself, and drawing a chair to the table,

123
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:16,917
“that you will not think I spoke harshly to you just now. I had no intention of doing it, and I am

124
00:11:16,917 --> 00:11:24,199
sorry for it if I did. I wish you well and happy!” As I put my glass to my lips, he glanced with

125
00:11:24,199 --> 00:11:29,436
surprise at the end of his neckerchief, dropping from his mouth when he opened it, and stretched

126
00:11:29,438 --> 00:11:37,718
out his hand. I gave him mine, and then he drank, and drew his sleeve across his eyes and forehead.

127
00:11:37,718 --> 00:11:41,277
“How are you living?” I asked him.

128
00:11:41,277 --> 00:11:47,000
“I’ve been a sheep-farmer, stock-breeder, other trades besides, away in the new world,” said he;

129
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
“many a thousand mile of stormy water off from this.”

130
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,078
“I hope you have done well?”

131
00:11:53,078 --> 00:11:57,917
“I’ve done wonderfully well. There’s others went out alonger me as has done well too,

132
00:11:57,917 --> 00:12:02,597
but no man has done nigh as well as me. I’m famous for it.”

133
00:12:02,597 --> 00:12:04,559
“I am glad to hear it.”

134
00:12:04,559 --> 00:12:08,116
“I hope to hear you say so, my dear boy.”

135
00:12:08,116 --> 00:12:12,839
Without stopping to try to understand those words or the tone in which they were spoken,

136
00:12:12,839 --> 00:12:17,319
I turned off to a point that had just come into my mind.

137
00:12:17,319 --> 00:12:23,678
“Have you ever seen a messenger you once sent to me,” I inquired, “since he undertook that trust?”

138
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:27,800
“Never set eyes upon him. I warn’t likely to it.”

139
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:33,680
“He came faithfully, and he brought me the two one-pound notes. I was a poor boy then,

140
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:38,559
as you know, and to a poor boy they were a little fortune. But, like you,

141
00:12:38,559 --> 00:12:42,718
I have done well since, and you must let me pay them back. You

142
00:12:42,718 --> 00:12:49,277
can put them to some other poor boy’s use.” I took out my purse.

143
00:12:49,277 --> 00:12:54,358
He watched me as I laid my purse upon the table and opened it, and he watched me as I separated

144
00:12:54,358 --> 00:13:00,480
two one-pound notes from its contents. They were clean and new, and I spread them out and handed

145
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:06,838
them over to him. Still watching me, he laid them one upon the other, folded them long-wise,

146
00:13:06,839 --> 00:13:13,038
gave them a twist, set fire to them at the lamp, and dropped the ashes into the tray.

147
00:13:13,038 --> 00:13:18,199
“May I make so bold,” he said then, with a smile that was like a frown,

148
00:13:18,199 --> 00:13:22,957
and with a frown that was like a smile, “as ask you how you have done well,

149
00:13:22,957 --> 00:13:27,718
since you and me was out on them lone shivering marshes?”

150
00:13:27,718 --> 00:13:29,038
“How?”

151
00:13:29,038 --> 00:13:30,038
“Ah!”

152
00:13:30,038 --> 00:13:34,038
He emptied his glass, got up, and stood at the side of the fire,

153
00:13:34,038 --> 00:13:40,559
with his heavy brown hand on the mantel-shelf. He put a foot up to the bars, to dry and warm it,

154
00:13:40,559 --> 00:13:46,519
and the wet boot began to steam; but, he neither looked at it, nor at the fire,

155
00:13:46,519 --> 00:13:53,038
but steadily looked at me. It was only now that I began to tremble.

156
00:13:53,038 --> 00:13:58,879
When my lips had parted, and had shaped some words that were without sound, I forced myself to tell

157
00:13:58,879 --> 00:14:05,957
him (though I could not do it distinctly), that I had been chosen to succeed to some property.

158
00:14:05,957 --> 00:14:09,879
“Might a mere warmint ask what property?” said he.

159
00:14:09,879 --> 00:14:13,038
I faltered, “I don’t know.”

160
00:14:13,038 --> 00:14:17,398
“Might a mere warmint ask whose property?” said he.

161
00:14:17,398 --> 00:14:20,957
I faltered again, “I don’t know.”

162
00:14:20,957 --> 00:14:24,519
“Could I make a guess, I wonder,” said the Convict,

163
00:14:24,519 --> 00:14:31,718
“at your income since you come of age! As to the first figure now. Five?”

164
00:14:31,718 --> 00:14:37,237
With my heart beating like a heavy hammer of disordered action, I rose out of my chair,

165
00:14:37,237 --> 00:14:40,758
and stood with my hand upon the back of it, looking wildly at him.

166
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:46,678
“Concerning a guardian,” he went on. “There ought to have been some guardian,

167
00:14:46,680 --> 00:14:50,438
or such-like, whiles you was a minor. Some lawyer,

168
00:14:50,438 --> 00:14:56,398
maybe. As to the first letter of that lawyer’s name now. Would it be J?”

169
00:14:56,398 --> 00:15:01,358
All the truth of my position came flashing on me; and its disappointments, dangers,

170
00:15:01,358 --> 00:15:05,957
disgraces, consequences of all kinds, rushed in in such a multitude that I

171
00:15:05,957 --> 00:15:10,879
was borne down by them and had to struggle for every breath I drew.

172
00:15:10,879 --> 00:15:15,519
“Put it,” he resumed, “as the employer of that lawyer whose name begun with a J,

173
00:15:15,519 --> 00:15:20,839
and might be Jaggers,—put it as he had come over sea to Portsmouth, and had landed there,

174
00:15:20,839 --> 00:15:26,160
and had wanted to come on to you. ‘However, you have found me out,’ you says just now.

175
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:31,758
Well! However, did I find you out? Why, I wrote from Portsmouth to a person in London,

176
00:15:31,758 --> 00:15:37,957
for particulars of your address. That person’s name? Why, Wemmick.”

177
00:15:37,957 --> 00:15:43,000
I could not have spoken one word, though it had been to save my life. I stood, with a hand on

178
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,078
the chair-back and a hand on my breast, where I seemed to be suffocating,—I stood so, looking

179
00:15:48,078 --> 00:15:54,480
wildly at him, until I grasped at the chair, when the room began to surge and turn. He caught me,

180
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:59,957
drew me to the sofa, put me up against the cushions, and bent on one knee before me,

181
00:15:59,957 --> 00:16:07,199
bringing the face that I now well remembered, and that I shuddered at, very near to mine.

182
00:16:07,199 --> 00:16:13,436
“Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you! It’s me wot has done it! I swore that time,

183
00:16:13,438 --> 00:16:19,038
sure as ever I earned a guinea, that guinea should go to you. I swore arterwards, sure

184
00:16:19,038 --> 00:16:25,879
as ever I spec’lated and got rich, you should get rich. I lived rough, that you should live smooth;

185
00:16:25,879 --> 00:16:31,800
I worked hard, that you should be above work. What odds, dear boy? Do I tell it,

186
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:38,758
fur you to feel a obligation? Not a bit. I tell it, fur you to know as that there hunted dunghill

187
00:16:38,758 --> 00:16:45,559
dog wot you kep life in, got his head so high that he could make a gentleman,—and, Pip, you’re him!”

188
00:16:45,559 --> 00:16:50,641
The abhorrence in which I held the man, the dread I had of him, the repugnance with

189
00:16:50,641 --> 00:16:57,116
which I shrank from him, could not have been exceeded if he had been some terrible beast.

190
00:16:57,116 --> 00:17:04,038
“Look’ee here, Pip. I’m your second father. You’re my son,—more to me nor any son. I’ve

191
00:17:04,038 --> 00:17:10,319
put away money, only for you to spend. When I was a hired-out shepherd in a solitary hut,

192
00:17:10,319 --> 00:17:15,480
not seeing no faces but faces of sheep till I half forgot wot men’s and women’s faces wos like,

193
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:21,358
I see yourn. I drops my knife many a time in that hut when I was a-eating my dinner or my supper,

194
00:17:21,358 --> 00:17:27,557
and I says, ‘Here’s the boy again, a looking at me whiles I eats and drinks!’ I see you

195
00:17:27,559 --> 00:17:33,398
there a many times, as plain as ever I see you on them misty marshes. ‘Lord strike me dead!’

196
00:17:33,398 --> 00:17:38,877
I says each time,—and I goes out in the air to say it under the open heavens,—‘but wot,

197
00:17:38,879 --> 00:17:43,839
if I gets liberty and money, I’ll make that boy a gentleman!’ And I done it. Why,

198
00:17:43,839 --> 00:17:47,319
look at you, dear boy! Look at these here lodgings of yourn,

199
00:17:47,319 --> 00:17:55,398
fit for a lord! A lord? Ah! You shall show money with lords for wagers, and beat ’em!”

200
00:17:55,398 --> 00:18:00,277
In his heat and triumph, and in his knowledge that I had been nearly fainting,

201
00:18:00,277 --> 00:18:06,358
he did not remark on my reception of all this. It was the one grain of relief I had.

202
00:18:06,358 --> 00:18:10,718
“Look’ee here!” he went on, taking my watch out of my pocket, and turning

203
00:18:10,718 --> 00:18:15,758
towards him a ring on my finger, while I recoiled from his touch as if he had been a snake,

204
00:18:15,758 --> 00:18:22,116
“a gold ’un and a beauty: that’s a gentleman’s, I hope! A diamond all set round with rubies;

205
00:18:22,116 --> 00:18:27,917
that’s a gentleman’s, I hope! Look at your linen; fine and beautiful! Look at your clothes;

206
00:18:27,917 --> 00:18:32,917
better ain’t to be got! And your books too,” turning his eyes round the room, “mounting up,

207
00:18:32,917 --> 00:18:37,319
on their shelves, by hundreds! And you read ’em; don’t you? I see you’d been a reading

208
00:18:37,319 --> 00:18:42,160
of ’em when I come in. Ha, ha, ha! You shall read ’em to me, dear boy! And if they’re in

209
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:46,879
foreign languages wot I don’t understand, I shall be just as proud as if I did.”

210
00:18:46,879 --> 00:18:52,277
Again he took both my hands and put them to his lips, while my blood ran cold within me.

211
00:18:52,277 --> 00:18:56,319
“Don’t you mind talking, Pip,” said he, after again drawing his sleeve over his

212
00:18:56,319 --> 00:19:00,839
eyes and forehead, as the click came in his throat which I well remembered,—and

213
00:19:00,839 --> 00:19:04,597
he was all the more horrible to me that he was so much in earnest;

214
00:19:04,597 --> 00:19:09,839
“you can’t do better nor keep quiet, dear boy. You ain’t looked slowly forward to this as I have;

215
00:19:09,839 --> 00:19:15,038
you wosn’t prepared for this as I wos. But didn’t you never think it might be me?”

216
00:19:15,038 --> 00:19:18,116
“O no, no, no,” I returned, “Never, never!”

217
00:19:18,116 --> 00:19:25,000
“Well, you see it wos me, and single-handed. Never a soul in it but my own self and Mr. Jaggers.”

218
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,480
“Was there no one else?” I asked.

219
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:33,756
“No,” said he, with a glance of surprise: “who else should there be? And, dear boy,

220
00:19:33,758 --> 00:19:35,879
how good looking you have growed! There’s bright

221
00:19:35,879 --> 00:19:40,438
eyes somewheres—eh? Isn’t there bright eyes somewheres, wot you love the thoughts on?”

222
00:19:41,319 --> 00:19:44,839
O Estella, Estella!

223
00:19:44,839 --> 00:19:49,358
“They shall be yourn, dear boy, if money can buy ’em. Not that a gentleman like you,

224
00:19:49,358 --> 00:19:55,277
so well set up as you, can’t win ’em off of his own game; but money shall back you! Let

225
00:19:55,277 --> 00:20:01,160
me finish wot I was a telling you, dear boy. From that there hut and that there hiring-out,

226
00:20:01,160 --> 00:20:06,597
I got money left me by my master (which died, and had been the same as me), and got my liberty and

227
00:20:06,597 --> 00:20:13,160
went for myself. In every single thing I went for, I went for you. ‘Lord strike a blight upon it,’ I

228
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:21,480
says, wotever it was I went for, ‘if it ain’t for him!’ It all prospered wonderful. As I giv’ you to

229
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:28,078
understand just now, I’m famous for it. It was the money left me, and the gains of the first few year

230
00:20:28,078 --> 00:20:35,758
wot I sent home to Mr. Jaggers—all for you—when he first come arter you, agreeable to my letter.”

231
00:20:35,758 --> 00:20:40,519
O that he had never come! That he had left me at the forge,—far

232
00:20:40,519 --> 00:20:47,116
from contented, yet, by comparison happy! “And then, dear boy, it was a recompense to me,

233
00:20:47,116 --> 00:20:53,199
look’ee here, to know in secret that I was making a gentleman. The blood horses of them colonists

234
00:20:53,199 --> 00:21:00,199
might fling up the dust over me as I was walking; what do I say? I says to myself, ‘I’m making a

235
00:21:00,199 --> 00:21:06,358
better gentleman nor ever you’ll be!’ When one of ’em says to another, ‘He was a convict, a few

236
00:21:06,358 --> 00:21:13,756
year ago, and is a ignorant common fellow now, for all he’s lucky,’ what do I say? I says to myself,

237
00:21:13,758 --> 00:21:19,160
‘If I ain’t a gentleman, nor yet ain’t got no learning, I’m the owner of such. All on you owns

238
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:26,480
stock and land; which on you owns a brought-up London gentleman?’ This way I kep myself a-going.

239
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:31,038
And this way I held steady afore my mind that I would for certain come one day and see my boy,

240
00:21:31,038 --> 00:21:37,398
and make myself known to him, on his own ground.” He laid his hand on my shoulder. I shuddered at

241
00:21:37,398 --> 00:21:42,557
the thought that for anything I knew, his hand might be stained with blood.

242
00:21:42,559 --> 00:21:45,358
“It warn’t easy, Pip, for me to leave them parts,

243
00:21:45,358 --> 00:21:51,639
nor yet it warn’t safe. But I held to it, and the harder it was, the stronger I held,

244
00:21:51,641 --> 00:22:00,800
for I was determined, and my mind firm made up. At last I done it. Dear boy, I done it!”

245
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:04,519
I tried to collect my thoughts, but I was stunned. Throughout,

246
00:22:04,519 --> 00:22:09,641
I had seemed to myself to attend more to the wind and the rain than to him; even now,

247
00:22:09,641 --> 00:22:16,000
I could not separate his voice from those voices, though those were loud and his was silent.

248
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:21,519
“Where will you put me?” he asked, presently. “I must be put somewheres, dear boy.”

249
00:22:21,519 --> 00:22:23,917
“To sleep?” said I.

250
00:22:23,917 --> 00:22:27,199
“Yes. And to sleep long and sound,” he answered;

251
00:22:27,199 --> 00:22:31,398
“for I’ve been sea-tossed and sea-washed, months and months.”

252
00:22:31,398 --> 00:22:38,277
“My friend and companion,” said I, rising from the sofa, “is absent; you must have his room.”

253
00:22:38,277 --> 00:22:40,237
“He won’t come back to-morrow; will he?”

254
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:48,038
“No,” said I, answering almost mechanically, in spite of my utmost efforts; “not to-morrow.”

255
00:22:48,038 --> 00:22:50,800
“Because, look’ee here, dear boy,” he said, dropping his voice,

256
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:56,917
and laying a long finger on my breast in an impressive manner, “caution is necessary.”

257
00:22:56,917 --> 00:22:59,597
“How do you mean? Caution?”

258
00:22:59,597 --> 00:23:02,597
“By G——, it’s Death!”

259
00:23:02,597 --> 00:23:04,319
“What’s death?”

260
00:23:04,319 --> 00:23:10,398
“I was sent for life. It’s death to come back. There’s been overmuch coming back of late years,

261
00:23:10,398 --> 00:23:13,917
and I should of a certainty be hanged if took.”

262
00:23:13,917 --> 00:23:19,398
Nothing was needed but this; the wretched man, after loading wretched me with his

263
00:23:19,398 --> 00:23:23,756
gold and silver chains for years, had risked his life to come to me,

264
00:23:23,758 --> 00:23:29,116
and I held it there in my keeping! If I had loved him instead of abhorring him;

265
00:23:29,116 --> 00:23:34,480
if I had been attracted to him by the strongest admiration and affection, instead of shrinking

266
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:41,678
from him with the strongest repugnance; it could have been no worse. On the contrary, it would

267
00:23:41,680 --> 00:23:50,438
have been better, for his preservation would then have naturally and tenderly addressed my heart.

268
00:23:50,438 --> 00:23:55,199
My first care was to close the shutters, so that no light might be seen from without,

269
00:23:55,199 --> 00:24:01,480
and then to close and make fast the doors. While I did so, he stood at the table drinking rum

270
00:24:01,480 --> 00:24:07,436
and eating biscuit; and when I saw him thus engaged, I saw my convict on the marshes at

271
00:24:07,438 --> 00:24:15,160
his meal again. It almost seemed to me as if he must stoop down presently, to file at his leg.

272
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:19,237
When I had gone into Herbert’s room, and had shut off any other communication between it

273
00:24:19,237 --> 00:24:23,678
and the staircase than through the room in which our conversation had been held,

274
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:28,641
I asked him if he would go to bed? He said yes, but asked me for some of my

275
00:24:28,641 --> 00:24:34,000
“gentleman’s linen” to put on in the morning. I brought it out, and laid it ready for him,

276
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:39,597
and my blood again ran cold when he again took me by both hands to give me good-night.

277
00:24:39,597 --> 00:24:44,319
I got away from him, without knowing how I did it, and mended the fire in the room

278
00:24:44,319 --> 00:24:51,038
where we had been together, and sat down by it, afraid to go to bed. For an hour or more,

279
00:24:51,038 --> 00:24:56,480
I remained too stunned to think; and it was not until I began to think, that I began fully to

280
00:24:56,480 --> 00:25:02,116
know how wrecked I was, and how the ship in which I had sailed was gone to pieces.

281
00:25:02,116 --> 00:25:09,000
Miss Havisham’s intentions towards me, all a mere dream; Estella not designed for me;

282
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,917
I only suffered in Satis House as a convenience, a sting for the greedy

283
00:25:12,917 --> 00:25:18,559
relations, a model with a mechanical heart to practise on when no other practice was at hand;

284
00:25:18,559 --> 00:25:25,597
those were the first smarts I had. But, sharpest and deepest pain of all,—it was for the convict,

285
00:25:25,597 --> 00:25:31,480
guilty of I knew not what crimes, and liable to be taken out of those rooms where I sat thinking,

286
00:25:31,480 --> 00:25:36,718
and hanged at the Old Bailey door, that I had deserted Joe.

287
00:25:36,718 --> 00:25:42,116
I would not have gone back to Joe now, I would not have gone back to Biddy now, for any

288
00:25:42,116 --> 00:25:47,879
consideration; simply, I suppose, because my sense of my own worthless conduct to them was greater

289
00:25:47,879 --> 00:25:53,559
than every consideration. No wisdom on earth could have given me the comfort that I should

290
00:25:53,559 --> 00:26:02,000
have derived from their simplicity and fidelity; but I could never, never, undo what I had done.

291
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:07,116
In every rage of wind and rush of rain, I heard pursuers. Twice, I could have sworn

292
00:26:07,116 --> 00:26:11,199
there was a knocking and whispering at the outer door. With these fears upon me,

293
00:26:11,199 --> 00:26:16,800
I began either to imagine or recall that I had had mysterious warnings of this man’s approach. That,

294
00:26:16,800 --> 00:26:21,758
for weeks gone by, I had passed faces in the streets which I had thought like his.

295
00:26:21,758 --> 00:26:26,038
That these likenesses had grown more numerous, as he, coming over the sea,

296
00:26:26,038 --> 00:26:31,237
had drawn nearer. That his wicked spirit had somehow sent these messengers to mine,

297
00:26:31,237 --> 00:26:36,800
and that now on this stormy night he was as good as his word, and with me.

298
00:26:36,800 --> 00:26:41,398
Crowding up with these reflections came the reflection that I had seen him with my childish

299
00:26:41,398 --> 00:26:47,436
eyes to be a desperately violent man; that I had heard that other convict reiterate that he had

300
00:26:47,438 --> 00:26:53,000
tried to murder him; that I had seen him down in the ditch tearing and fighting like a wild

301
00:26:53,000 --> 00:27:00,641
beast. Out of such remembrances I brought into the light of the fire a half-formed terror that it

302
00:27:00,641 --> 00:27:07,038
might not be safe to be shut up there with him in the dead of the wild solitary night. This dilated

303
00:27:07,038 --> 00:27:14,199
until it filled the room, and impelled me to take a candle and go in and look at my dreadful burden.

304
00:27:14,199 --> 00:27:20,718
He had rolled a handkerchief round his head, and his face was set and lowering in his sleep. But

305
00:27:20,718 --> 00:27:28,398
he was asleep, and quietly too, though he had a pistol lying on the pillow. Assured of this,

306
00:27:28,398 --> 00:27:33,480
I softly removed the key to the outside of his door, and turned it on him before

307
00:27:33,480 --> 00:27:40,358
I again sat down by the fire. Gradually I slipped from the chair and lay on the floor.

308
00:27:40,358 --> 00:27:45,557
When I awoke without having parted in my sleep with the perception of my wretchedness,

309
00:27:45,559 --> 00:27:50,358
the clocks of the Eastward churches were striking five, the candles were wasted out,

310
00:27:50,358 --> 00:27:57,038
the fire was dead, and the wind and rain intensified the thick black darkness.

311
00:27:57,038 --> 00:28:01,000
This is the end of the second stage of Pip’s expectations.
